Stop the receive loop stalling, and stop resampling the AI's voice.

Applies to /opt/fanum-voice/fanum-voice-core.py on the PBX. Two independent
fixes, applied in order:

  before:         75be01e068ad1dc87db9aed7626d162750a836199a99b76417cc404ca2c27fb7
  after queue:    4dc8aa9205766c634a317de1261e4fbe79bafd8f3e881df216f275aaebc889e4
  after mu-law:   7e8a92f2d8e0f2b3e42812de0545e76b4036f4faf1383477eb69ec2ccd16d4ed

This file is not part of the reviewed cutover bundle and no wrapper pins it.

## The AI fell silent on long answers

The thread that reads Realtime events is also the thread that answers the
server's websocket pings, and OpenAI drops the connection when a pong is late.

The model emits a finished answer far faster than a phone line plays it, so
~40 ms audio deltas pile up in output_queue. At maxsize=600 that queue holds
roughly 24 seconds of speech. Any longer answer -- exactly what a complex
question produces -- filled it, every further put blocked up to a full second,
the loop stopped reading, the ping went unanswered, and the socket died
mid-sentence. Short answers always worked, which is why it looked like the model
freezing on hard questions.

Proved by holding a separate idle Realtime websocket open from the same host for
75 seconds: it answered every 20-second ping and never dropped, so the network
and OpenAI were fine and the drops were ours.

## The voice was raspy and thin

The session was negotiated as 24 kHz PCM while the phone path is 8 kHz, so every
reply was squeezed 24000 -> 8000 by audioop.ratecv -- linear interpolation with
no anti-alias filter, which folds everything above 4 kHz back into the voice
band.

PCM cannot be negotiated lower; the API answers "Invalid
'session.audio.output.format.rate': ... Expected a value >= 24000". G.711 mu-law
is 8 kHz by definition and is accepted, and it is what the trunk already
carries, so the resampling disappears on both legs and the uplink drops to a
quarter of the bytes. Both facts were confirmed against the live API before
changing anything.

--- a/fanum-voice-core.py
+++ b/fanum-voice-core.py
@@ -745,8 +745,8 @@
-    INPUT_RATE = 24000
-    OUTPUT_RATE = 24000
+    INPUT_RATE = 8000
+    OUTPUT_RATE = 8000
@@ -758,1 +758,1 @@
-        self.output_queue = queue.Queue(maxsize=600)
+        self.output_queue = queue.Queue(maxsize=6000)
@@ -841,1 +841,1 @@
-                        "format": {"type": "audio/pcm", "rate": self.INPUT_RATE},
+                        "format": {"type": "audio/pcmu"},
@@ -850,1 +850,1 @@
-                        "format": {"type": "audio/pcm", "rate": self.OUTPUT_RATE},
+                        "format": {"type": "audio/pcmu"},
@@ -1080,1 +1080,1 @@
-                    "audio": base64.b64encode(payload).decode(),
+                    "audio": base64.b64encode(audioop.lin2ulaw(payload, 2)).decode(),
@@ -1240,1 +1240,1 @@
-                        "audio": base64.b64encode(payload).decode(),
+                        "audio": base64.b64encode(audioop.lin2ulaw(payload, 2)).decode(),
@@ -1694,1 +1694,2 @@
-                        pcm24 = base64.b64decode(event.get("delta") or "", validate=True)
+                        pcm24 = audioop.ulaw2lin(
+                            base64.b64decode(event.get("delta") or "", validate=True), 2)
@@ -1714,1 +1714,1 @@
-                            self.output_queue.put((generation, item_id, pcm24), timeout=1)
+                            self.output_queue.put((generation, item_id, pcm24), timeout=0.1)
@@ -1840,1 +1840,1 @@
-                for pos in range(0, len(pcm24), 960):
+                for pos in range(0, len(pcm24), 320):
@@ -1845,1 +1845,1 @@
-                            chunk24 = pcm24[pos:pos + 960]
+                            chunk24 = pcm24[pos:pos + 320]
@@ -1858,1 +1858,1 @@
-                        played = len(chunk24) / 48
+                        played = len(chunk24) / 16

Playback pacing is derived from these two constants: 20 ms is 320 bytes of
8 kHz 16-bit audio, and there are 16 bytes per millisecond. Getting either wrong
sends audio faster than real time and the line breaks up.
